{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 754 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "91b9cb6c3827d8c2eb832a50d55440dd4edc2a8e5067760e7c8c6bd946385869" :edb/db-before-t 753 :edb/db-after-t 754 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534066 50 #inst "2026-10-06T02:39:27.235Z" 13194139534066 true] [17592186129035 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276304 13194139534066 true] [17592186129036 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276311 13194139534066 true] [17592186129037 1020 #edb/ref 17592186276297 13194139534066 true] [17592186129338 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534066 true] [17592186129338 1100 "What is the domain of the function  $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186129338 1100 "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = 2\\cot (3x)$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 false] [17592186129338 1145 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186129518 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534066 true] [17592186129518 1100 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\tan (3x) + 2$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 false] [17592186129518 1100 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186129518 1145 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1017 #edb/ref 17592186276298 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1089 "q-10406" 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1100 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1113 #uuid "ff49aec1-dbe8-567d-97c7-2ab5d0eb085f" 13194139534066 true] [17592186276297 1145 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276299 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276300 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276301 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276302 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1000 #edb/ref 17592186276303 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1041 "selection" 13194139534066 true] [17592186276298 1104 #edb/ref 17592186276299 13